/**
 * Stage 1.3T-W - source of truth for the public Translation Information page.
 *
 * The `translation*` sentences below are the SAME hand-written translations the
 * application ships in lib/l10n/app_<locale>.arb, copied verbatim so the website
 * and the app cannot describe the feature differently in the same language.
 * Nothing here was machine-translated and nothing is an English placeholder.
 *
 * The `web*` keys have no counterpart in the application's ARB files — they are
 * website-only sentences, hand-localized here for the same 19 locales.
 *
 * Stage 1.6W replaced `webNotYet` ("Connections and message translation are not
 * available yet") with `webGradualRollout`. The old sentence was a "coming soon"
 * claim, and the application repository's README rejects that framing for a
 * specific reason: a store reviewer who reads a page describing a feature they
 * cannot find in the build will reject the submission. The replacement is true
 * before review, during review, during a staged rollout and after a partial
 * rollout, so it never has to be edited at the moment a server flag changes —
 * which is exactly the coordination that fails. It claims neither that the
 * feature is enabled nor that it reaches every account, and it names no app
 * version. Authority: moment_app README, "Website and store sequencing".
 *
 * PROTECTED GOOGLE TEXT - deliberately identical in every locale:
 *
 *   - GOOGLE_TRANSLATE_WORDMARK ("Google Translate") and TRANSLATE_ACTION
 *     ("Translate with Google") are Google word marks. Google's naming rules
 *     forbid modifying a mark "for example, through hyphenation, combination
 *     abbreviation, or acronym", so no locale gets a translated form: Google
 *     ships none, and inventing one would reshape the mark.
 *   - DISCLAIMER is the notice Google requires to be reproduced exactly. It is
 *     not translated, re-cased, abridged or paraphrased in any locale. The
 *     application carries the identical string, and all 19 application locales
 *     already hold exactly one distinct value for it.
 *
 * That is why one identical English run of text legitimately appears on all 20
 * routes. tests/translation-pages.test.js asserts it stays identical.
 */

